Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 05:17:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] =?UTF-8?q?robo(entry/records/downloads):=20Shift=20on?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20every=20slab=20=E2=80=94=20picked=20on=20entry,=20shown=20in?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20the=20register,=20in=20the=20sheets?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The register has always had a Shift column and the ERP had nowhere to put
one. RoboShift.shiftNumber looks like the place and is not: that row is
plumbing the schema requires, created silently ONCE A DAY the first time
the tablet is opened and stamped from the wall clock, then shared by every
batch and every slab of that day. A day opened at 07:00 said "1" on a slab
made at 23:40, and a register typed up the next morning said the typist's
shift for the whole night before. That is why Shift had been taken out of
Complete Details and both Excel sheets — the number was a record of
nothing. This adds the thing it was mistaken for.

- productionShift (new, pure): the three shifts the plant runs — A 06:00,
  B 14:00, C 22:00, C wrapping midnight — each boundary belonging to the
  shift it starts, so 14:00 is B's first minute. productionShiftOf() reads
  a slab's shift: the stored one, else the one its In Time falls in, else
  its Out Time, else "". parseShiftCode() is the one way a value gets in,
  accepting "A", "Shift A", the whole label and the 1/2/3 an older register
  writes, and refusing everything else rather than guessing.
- schema: RoboProductionRecord.productionShift, nullable text, "A"/"B"/"C"
  or NULL and never "" — the same shape and the same reason as the
  per-slab productionDate beside it. Added to Neon out-of-band as usual
  (ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN, additive, nothing rewritten).
- NOTHING WAS BACK-FILLED, and nothing is written back on read. The 5,780
  slabs already in the register keep a NULL column and report the shift
  their own In Time falls in — on the real data that is 1,627 A, 2,035 B,
  2,117 C and one slab with no time at all, and a full day reads ~60/65/65
  the way the line actually runs. The moment an operator picks a shift,
  that is the answer for that slab.
- Robo Entry: a Shift dropdown beside Production Date, the two of them the
  first row. It FOLLOWS THE IN TIME typed below — 22:10 in is Shift C
  whatever the tablet was showing — until the operator picks one by hand,
  which then stands for that slab; a save clears the flag so the next slab
  follows its own In Time and a handover corrects itself. Carried forward
  between slabs like the date. Editing a slab keeps the shift it is shown
  with, so correcting something else cannot move it.
- Slab Records: a Shift column between Production Date and Design Name,
  and the last column's heading is now Review (the Complete Details button
  under it is untouched).
- Complete Details: the slab's Shift beside its Production Date.
- Excel: Shift immediately after Production Date on Complete Production
  (one row per slab) and on Delay List (the shift of the slab the delay
  held up), with the widths to match. The Production Setup and Date-wise
  Totals sheets are left alone — neither has one slab behind a row. The
  register importer reads a Shift column back, in letters or in 1/2/3, so
  a sheet exported from here and loaded again keeps its shifts.
- The dropdown reads "Shift C — 22:00–06:00" rather than "22:00 to 06:00"
  for a measured reason: on a tablet in portrait the box is 224px and a
  touch screen renders at 16px, where the spelt-out version came to 197px
  against 186px of room and clipped. Checked rendered at 390, 810, 1180
  and 1440px — no clipping, no sideways scroll, 44px tap targets.
- Reports are untouched, as asked.
- tests: the three windows minute by minute (every minute owned exactly
  once), the handover boundaries, the midnight wrap, every spelling a
  stored or typed value can take, stored-beats-derived, In-Time-beats-
  Out-Time, a slab with nothing at all, a read never mutating, and source
  guards that both write paths validate, that all four screens read the one
  resolver, that Slab Records has Shift in the right place with Review last
  and a matching colSpan, and that the importer writes it back.
  Mutation-checked.

