Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The hourly line was one blue trend across a run that is in fact three shifts
repeating. Nothing on the chart said where A ended and B began, so a reader
comparing "the night" against "the morning" had to count hours along the
axis and remember 06:00 / 14:00 / 22:00 themselves.

It is still ONE line over the selected batch's own run, with the same batch
filter, the same hourly counts by Out Time, the same timeline from the first
slab's In to the last slab's Out, the same zero hours kept and the same
midnight handling. What changed is what it says.

- hourlyShifts (new, pure): the shift of an HOUR — A 06:00-14:00, B 14:00-22:00,
  C 22:00-06:00, each boundary belonging to the shift it starts, so 14:00 is
  B's first hour and not A's last. The windows are the plant's own, the same
  three productionShift.ts defines for a slab. shiftBlocks() splits the run
  into consecutive same-shift stretches (the night shift stays ONE block across
  midnight) and shiftSpans() turns those into colour stops.
- The colour is a hard-stopped gradient on the single path, NOT three series.
  Three series was the obvious way and the wrong one: each would end where the
  next began, so every boundary gained a seam, the monotone curve was re-fitted
  per piece and one trend grew three legend entries. Two stops share each
  boundary offset, so the colour turns at a point and never blends. Offsets are
  fractions of the path's own box — point i at i/(n-1) — so nothing here knows
  about margins, axis width or padding, and the colours cannot drift out of
  step with the plot at any width.
- The boundary sits halfway between the last hour of one shift and the first of
  the next, which is exactly 06:00 / 14:00 / 22:00 — not a point early or late.
- Red, dark blue, dark green, as asked, but at measured steps: #c62828 against
  #1b5e20 is ΔE 2.0 under protanopia, i.e. the same colour to a red-green
  colour-blind reader. Lightened until they part (#cf2f26 / #1f4fa8 / #1a7a42)
  every check passes — lightness band, chroma, CVD separation at ΔE 8.5,
  contrast on the white card.
- A band under the time labels names each stretch: SHIFT A / SHIFT B / SHIFT C,
  bold capitals at 14px in the shift's own colour, centred on the hours it
  covers and repeating as the run carries on into the next day. It is a second
  X-axis, so it lives in the chart's coordinate space and stays aligned at any
  width, scrolled or not. A one-hour block (a run ending on a shift's first
  hour) shows the letter alone rather than colliding with its neighbours. The
  names carry the same information as the colours, so the chart is readable
  with no colour vision at all.
- Date dividers: 2px and a longer dash in a darker grey, the date above them
  bold — on a two-day run at the old hairline they were lost among the
  gridlines.
- Both axes are now named: "Number of Slabs" above the scale and "Time Duration
  (in Hours)" centred below. Plain HTML outside the plot, so neither moves or
  is clipped when a long run scrolls under the pinned axis.
- Historical batches need nothing: the shift of an hour is read off the clock,
  so a run entered years before the Shift field existed is coloured exactly as
  correctly as one entered today, with nothing read from the database.
- tests: the boundaries hour by hour, every hour owned exactly once, absolute
  hours, the owner's 1465 example splitting 2/8/8/8/8/8/3, the night shift as
  one block across midnight, the spans meeting end to end with no seam, the
  boundary arithmetic, a single hour, the labels, and guards that the chart
  still draws ONE Line stroked with the gradient, that both X-axes share one
  padding, that the date rule is heavier than the grid, that both axes are
  named, and that the colours are the checked ones. Mutation-checked.

