Hovering an hour gave "Completed: 6 Slabs" and stopped there. The next
question is always the same one — what held the line up — and answering it
meant leaving Reports, downloading the Delay List and reading off the hours
by hand.

The tooltip now carries the hour's delays: each one's code, how long it ran
and the start-end the register holds, then the hour's total. An hour that
lost nothing says "No delays" and totals 0 minutes, which is a reading too.

- hourlyProduction takes the batch's delays alongside its slabs and puts each
  under the hour it BEGAN in, whole. Not split across the hours it ran
  through: the register holds one delay of one duration, and a third of it is
  a figure nobody can find in the Delay List. That is what makes the hour's
  total exactly the sum of the lines above it — 6 + 22 = 28, arithmetic a
  supervisor can check by eye. Delays are listed earliest first.
- A delay carries a bare HH:MM, so it needs a day, and the day is the one the
  chart DREW ITS SLAB ON — placeSlabs may have moved that slab, and a delay
  drawn 24 hours from the slab it held up would sit in an hour where nothing
  was happening. Of the slab's own day and the two either side, the one taken
  is whichever puts the delay nearest the slab's own In -> Out window. One
  rule, and it settles both awkward cases without a special case for either:
  a slab that went in at 23:50 and was held at 00:10 was held after midnight,
  and a delay that began at 16:00 on a slab that went in at 16:05 began five
  minutes early, not a day late. No start time, no hour — left out rather
  than guessed into one.
- hourly route: the delays of THIS batch's slabs, reached through the slab so
  the two can never land on different days. A delay logged without a slab
  belongs to no batch, exactly as the Delay List download treats it when a
  batch is typed. Only the four fields the tooltip shows are read — the code,
  not its description.
- The card is wider, because a code, a duration and a time range on one line
  do not fit the stock box and wrapping them made "RM11 - 22 minutes
  [16:33-16:55]" unreadable. It is capped against the viewport so it cannot
  run off a phone.
- On a touch screen the same reading opens UNDER the chart on a tap, not as a
  card beside the finger. The plot is wider than a phone and scrolls, and
  Recharts keeps its tooltip inside the CHART, not inside the screen — so for
  any hour in the right half of the view the card was partly off the edge,
  and reading it meant scrolling the chart, which moved it again. A panel
  below is always whole. A mouse keeps the floating card.
- The hourly counts, the timeline, the dates and the shift colours are
  untouched; a caller that passes no delays gets exactly the series it got
  before, with every hour reading none.
- tests: the owner's 16:00-17:00 example to the minute, all six of an hour's
  delays listed and totalled, an empty hour, ordering, a delay running past
  its hour counted whole, a delay with no start time, one outside the run, a
  delay following a slab the chart moved a day, a delay after midnight on a
  slab that crossed it, a slab-less delay on its own date and one with no
  date, the counts unchanged, and guards on the route's query, the tooltip's
  four lines and the touch panel. Mutation-checked.

